<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Zamrak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1333–1393</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Abu Abdallah Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Zamrak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Zamrak was a poet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> at the Nasrid court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> under Muhammad V. A pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Ibn al-Khatib</a>, he succeeded his teacher as chief secretary and later turned against him. His poems are inscribed on the walls and fountains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>, including the Court of the Lions, making the palace a setting for some of the last great Arabic poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>.</p></div>
